<!--
  Field of the polarized-NH3 target magnet (5 T, transverse = along hall +x), added ON TOP of the solenoid field
  (DD4hep sums all <field> overlays). Included by solid_SIDIS_NH3.xml after the solenoid field.
  Requires from the top level: TargetCenter_z (-350 cm).

  Map: ptarget_oxford2012_6m.dat, computed in this repository (magnet_field/coil_map.py) from the 14 coils of the
  Oxford Instruments 2012 design study (magnet_field/models/nh3_target_oxford2012.json, the numbers of GEMC's
  field/oxford_field.f): the Biot-Savart sum over the coils, no iron. 2D map, r 0..600 cm x longitudinal
  -600..600 cm, 1 cm grid, gauss, columns "r x Br Bx", header symmetry cylindrical-x (map axis = hall x).
  Reference values: B(0,0) = +49996.25 G along +x; 87.72 G on the magnet axis and -42.98 G in the mid plane 2 m
  from the target; 3.22 G and -1.61 G at 6 m, where the map ends; maximum 8.62 T in the windings.

  It replaces GEMC's oxford_ptarget.dat (field_NH3_target_gemc.xml), made from the same coils:
    * more than 5 cm from the windings the two agree within the 0.1 G rounding of that file (79 336 nodes);
    * that file ends 2 m from the target, where the field is still 43 to 88 G;
    * its radial component has the wrong sign where the field points towards the magnet axis (the gap between
      the two coil halves, r up to 38 cm, |x| up to 14 cm, up to 4.4 T): its Br always has the sign of x, which
      contradicts div B = 0 with its own axial component. magnet_field/README.md has the comparison.
  Caveats: (1) this is the Oxford design; the installed Scientific-Magnetics magnet (SM2023 geometry, default of
  target_NH3.xml) has no public map or coil data yet; (2) no iron: the yoke of the solenoid, 0.8 m downstream,
  would change the stray field in and behind it; (3) the 5 T field deflects the incoming and outgoing beam (no
  chicane is modelled); (4) needs SoLID_FieldMapB with header/symmetry support (map axis from the
  "cylindrical-x" header, translationCoord = map origin).

  The map is looked up in ./fieldmaps, then in ${DETECTOR_PATH}/fieldmaps, else downloaded there once from the
  fieldmaps-v2 release of solid_dd4hep; its sha256 is checked. Offline, or to make it yourself (python3 with
  numpy and scipy, 10 s; the result has the same sha256):
      magnet_field/coil_map.py magnet_field/models/nh3_target_oxford2012.json -o ${DETECTOR_PATH}/fieldmaps/ptarget_oxford2012_6m.dat
-->
<lccdd>
  <fields>
    <field type="SoLID_FieldMapB" name="NH3TargetField" field_type="magnetic" coord_type="BrBz"
           field_map="fieldmaps/ptarget_oxford2012_6m.dat"
           cache="${DETECTOR_PATH}/fieldmaps"
           url="https://github.com/JeffersonLab/solid_dd4hep/releases/download/fieldmaps-v2/ptarget_oxford2012_6m.dat"
           sha256="21c04d6ec3d0ccca6d5e3d0c0bef77a46e5978c578b5f4b807575f1d35c19cf8"
           hint="Generate it from a solid_dd4hep checkout: magnet_field/coil_map.py magnet_field/models/nh3_target_oxford2012.json -o ${DETECTOR_PATH}/fieldmaps/ptarget_oxford2012_6m.dat"
           scale="1.0">
      <dimensions>
        <translationCoord x="0*cm" y="0*cm" z="TargetCenter_z"/>
      </dimensions>
    </field>
  </fields>
</lccdd>
